## Answer to the criteria: **No — not production ready.** 29 defects open (17 critical). Marker not written.

**What I did:** rooted `D-32NODE-SHARED-DIR-CREATE-PACE` and its tail `D-CRASH-CONSISTENCY-32-NOTERMINAL-354`, both open since 2026-07-29/08-02 and both previously chasing the wrong inode.

**Method — I stopped counting probes and started measuring wall.** Every prior session attacked this from probe *counts*, which say what the code did, not where the time went. I built `tests/mxfs_stackprof.py` + `tests/cc_stackprof.sh`: 20 Hz `/proc/PID/task/*/stack` sampling, histogrammed. No rebuild needed. 8 nodes, 34,300 samples of a virgin-fs run:

```
26.28%  mxfs_pal_cond_timedwait < caw_nudge_wait < caw_wait_for_grant
          < mxfs_dlm_caw_lock < mxfs_v5_dlm_inode_lock
16.59%  open_last_lookups < path_openat        (queued behind it, local i_rwsem)
39.61% + 13.59%  do_wait / pipe_read           (parent shells on those children)
```

Device path, CAS contention, AG locks: absent. ~100% of blocking wall is the CAW inode grant wait.

**The contended inode is the mount ROOT, ino 128** — not `.crash_consistency` (ino 29360256), which took exactly one >5 ms grant all run. That reframes the whole defect.

**The proof.** `P138-WAIT`/`P139`/`P204` turn out to be **always-on**, not `instr`-gated — I found that after `instr=1` perturbed a run from 86 s to >240 s. Twelve consecutive `P204-YT-DEFER` samples on test5, ino=128, spanning 3 seconds:

```
yt=20000  hpr=8209288  hex=0  wex=27da0000  age_ms=2037 → 4980
```

The yield ticket is frozen, **`hpr` is bit-identical for 3 full seconds — not one of the 7 PR holders releases under the EX BAST** — nobody holds EX, 10 nodes want it, and `age_ms` climbs to 4980 before the 5000 ms stale-ticket valve fires and it repeats. The system is running on its safety valve. All 202 root-inode waits are **PR readers** (mode 3), mean 3533 ms, `ffw_share` 100%, `doze250` p50=15 → 15 × 250 ms.

**Mechanism:** the BAST release path drains local ilock readers (`msleep < mxfs_drain_ilock_read < mxfs_dlm_bast_process`) while 32 nodes continuously path-walk the root, so readers arrive faster than the drain retires them — a read-side livelock with no barrier on new readers. Meanwhile every compatible PR requester is forced to defer on the foreign EX ticket that can never be satisfied.

**Two corrections to prior sessions' conclusions.** sess125's "empty shared directory" recipe was a correct *observation* with the wrong explanation — the virgin-fs `mkdir -p` is what puts an EX waiter on the *root*, and the directory persisting is why run two is fast. And sess24 had already documented half of this at `dlm_caw.c:155-245` ("defers purely on policy: strict writer preference, unbounded… yt_age is the wrong clock") without the other half: the guarded EX waiter can never be granted either.

Also: this run **passed at 86 s/90 s**, so the recipe is deterministic-*slow* (4–5× the warm 15–21 s), not deterministic-FAIL. The FAIL is whether the tail crosses budget.

Memory written and three ledger entries rewritten with the evidence, the fix shapes (read-side barrier first, then per-requester patience clock — `yt_age` resets on ticket rotation), a concrete PASS bar, and the `pkill -f` self-kill trap that cost me a cycle. Next session starts at the RULE-5 design consult, not at re-derivation.

